this is weird  we came in peace  for all your cash  hey guys pete here for all mankind is  back and today i'll be talking about the  season 3 premiere i was kind of mixed on  it there were some things i liked and  some things i didn't but it definitely  had its moments it was tense it was fun  to catch up with the characters years  later and there are a lot of interesting  details to parse as we skip forward into  the 90s in this alternate timeline i'll  be getting into everything that happened  and what i think about it right after  this quick spoiler warning if you  haven't watched episode 1 of for all  mankind's third season then this video  won't be for you and with that out of  the way let's get into it the thing that  pulled me into for all mankind's orbit  is this idea that history could be  drastically different if just a few key  events happen differently the main event  here is that the space race never ended  and that led to a series of changes that  managed to produce a reality that's more  hopeful than the one we're currently  living in i thought the closing shot of  this episode really encapsulated that  perfectly you have the wonders of space  and watching the sun rise as it comes  out from behind earth you have the  heroism from dany's actions that's  necessary in so many of these high  stress situations and you have the  fragility of it all as he suspended at  the end of that tether it's the  fragility of the positive changes in  this timeline the fragility of the  characters who are getting older and the  fragility of keeping things together in  a show that skips a decade in between  seasons episode 1 opens with a montage  catching us up on all that's happened in  between just like season two did because  this is all so crucial to how the story  is constructed i'll be doing a full  breakdown of the changes from our  timeline later this week that'll give me  a little time to watch all the minisodes  they added on apple tv that fill in the  gaps and read up some on the real events  that happened in our timeline the big  points that affect our understanding of  this episode are that gary hart avoided  his sex scandal and became the president  in 1984 after reagan completed his  second term in this timeline he's  re-elected in 1988 while ellen wilson is  elected as a republican senator in texas  in 1986. this sets up a race between her  and bill clinton in the opening year of  season 3  1992. this is when clinton became  president in our timeline but ellen is  the girl who caught the tank so it'll be  interesting to see just how much people  love astronauts in the show's timeline  things continue to develop at an  accelerated pace and there are mentions  of the new character deb iesa and his  partner richard hilliard making a  game-changing discovery related to clean  nuclear energy they've mastered nuclear  fusion which is a lot cleaner than the  fission technology we use in our  timeline but impractical due to  helium-3's rarity but then we also  learned that they discovered that to be  in abundance on the moon their company  will partner with nasa to mine it and we  see that this shift has drastically  reduced global warming karen partnered  with sam to start a space tourism  industry and danny stevens has followed  in his parents footsteps to become an  astronaut the focus on the continued  space race has been good for the soviet  union instead of collapsing they're  thriving under gorbachev who implemented  a hybrid socialist market economy their  influence is spreading throughout the  western hemisphere and now includes  mexico right on the u.s border space  travel is expanding to more countries  including india and china who have  aspirations for their own moon base and  north korea who scrapped a ballistic  missile program to focus on space in the  show's present we find karen baldwin on  the space hotel ship in orbit that her  company polaris belt which is where  episode 1 gets its name she gets a call  from her business partner sam who was  tracy's second husband after gordo who  we saw karen sell the outpost to at the  end of last season they're there getting  ready for the first group of guests to  arrive  and the commercial space tourism  industry is prepared to take its next  leap forward  after the credits there's another  installment in the margot waking up and  getting ready for work in her office  series this is how we met margot back in  1969 and in each new season we see that  her hard work is at least getting her a  more comfortable place to crash here in  1992 it becomes apparent that because  the show skips through time it has to  front load a lot of details into this  sequence to try to bring us up to speed  also margot has been working for nasa  for 30 years and she'll have been at jsc  for more or less that long before the  season ends so they have to introduce  new characters like the assistant who  emma is training margot is now the  director of nasa and it feels like this  will be the last season for her  generation of characters to be the  center of attention we get a quick look  at updated tech here as well she has a  flat panel display showing the mission  status board and nori has an apple pda  that looks much more advanced than the  newton message pad that existed at that  time but never really caught on in her  briefing we learned that debbayesa is  trying to curry favor with her and that  a north korean rocket has exploded in  orbit inside mars mission control we  learn that bill has become a flight  director and that they're testing a new  nuclear rocket engine on the moon it's  not working and if they can't fix it  they'll miss the 96 mars launch window  elena's on the project and she's going  to have to figure this out and we learn  later that she'll have to go to the moon  to try to apply her fix in person we  also learned that her father is now back  in the u.s living with her and her  husband and their son margot and her  staff also learned that russia has  announced that they're going to mars in  96 and her pal sergey delivers the party  line on tv saying that they'll be the  first to get there from that  announcement we learned that there's  pressure on margot to name their  commander which has come down to a  choice between danielle poole and ed  baldwin molly cobb is still the head of  the astronaut office and she appears to  have adjusted to losing her eyesight  margot tries to press her to announce  danielle as the mars commander but she's  leaning towards ed this disagreement  highlights the ways nasa is changing and  molly still thinks that someone with a  test pilot background like ed is the  better choice a little bit later margot  goes to pick up a record that her  assistant gave her a message about and  we learned that this is a code for her  to contact sergey he asked her for some  help and we learned that they've been  secretly helping each other over the  years he brings up this year's iac  conference which gives them a chance to  show that they have this affection for  each other and reminds us of the closing  moments of season 2 when the kgb was  watching his call to her she walks away  from the phone smiling thinking about  this conference and he gets a follow-up  call from his handlers telling him to  put more pressure on her to help them  with their nuclear engine which makes it  sound like they've announced this trip  to mars but they're not quite ready ed  and danny are both visiting the polaris  hotel for danny stevens wedding their  competition for the mars commander job  is lively and we see that they've both  been remarried and are traveling with  their significant others danny stevens  does marry his girlfriend amber and the  first ever wedding in space which turns  out to be pretty uncomfortable we learn  a lot of little details here like he's  gotten sober after his parents death  jimmy stevens makes things pretty  awkward with his best man speech where  he makes some accusations against nasa  we find out that kelly is a research  scientist in antarctica rather than a  pilot but we don't really get many more  details there and then things get really  uncomfortable when in the first dance  with his wife danny plays the version of  don't be cruel that he danced to with  karen when they were first starting  their affair this understandably freaks  karen out and i'm not sure what he was  going for here because everything had  been pointing to the fact that he'd  moved on and was pretty happy in this  new relationship we don't get to learn  anything more about it though because a  piece of debris from the north korean  rocket explosion hits one of the polaris  thrusters and that causes it to do an  uncommanded burn  this sets up an intense situation where  the gravity continues to increase and  things get worse when they send two  people out to do a repair and they get  killed after a cable breaks loose that  wreaks havoc on the ship as it thrashes  around and when they try to evacuate  everyone through the elevators sam is  killed ed injures his leg and things are  looking dire for anyone to be able to  get from the ring to the shuttles under  this amount of gravity the only path out  is to use an emergency access shaft  which is essentially a ladder and  thinking about trying to climb up that  at 2 g's your body feels like it weighs  twice as much and that just keeps going  up because ed is injured danny gets his  chance to be the hero and in a thrilling  sequence he goes out and is able to shut  the thruster down i thought this looked  fantastic and i mean i didn't really  think everyone was gonna die here but it  felt a little strange like it didn't  feel like danny had been developed as  this super astronaut or whatever we're  supposed to accept that he is here and i  suppose that's just part of what happens  when we have to jump over this large  period of time like if i compare this to  the season 2 premiere i really felt  something when molly left that lava tube  and went into the radiation to try to  save wubbo but as i mentioned i really  like that closing scene and it gets you  excited for all of the space stuff you  know is sure to come throughout the rest  of the season not a bad beginning a  pretty impressive set piece to close it  out but i do think it does raise some  concerns for the future i am a sucker  for all the alternate timeline stuff and  the ripple effects from the space race  continuing and i've grown attached to  the original cast of characters that  said it feels like they should be aging  out and i was surprised that we haven't  seen piscotty and sally ride or any of  the side characters from jamestown who  were all right there to help them  transition into the next generation  danny and jimmy stevens have this tragic  story related to gordo and tracy and  kelly could develop into a character in  her own right but it doesn't feel like  they could replace the originals based  on where we're at right now of course  the race to mars is on and there's tons  of potential there and we do know this  show generally gets better as things  progress both seasons have gone out with  amazing endings and i expect that this  one will too i think my biggest takeaway  re-watching them to get ready for this  is that the series does some things that  don't always work getting there but most  of it at least makes sense after they  deliver a properly dramatic climax of an  ending i think aleda is a pretty good  example of this in the first season a  lot of her firebug stuff felt so  disconnected from the main story but now  when i see her father is back living  with her as she's starting a family and  then i hear she's going to the moon it  all turns out to be the most emotionally  charged parts of this episode i hope  that karen and danny stuff eventually  make sense too i get that karen was  fairly unlikable and truly unhappy in  the first season and she went on this  path of self-discovery when you're  becoming something else it's very human  to make mistakes i mean that's sort of  what a midlife crisis is it just feels  so unnecessarily unsettling in their  connection to shane and danny's  childhood i feel like if gordo were  still around and had an affair with  kelly or if danielle poole had an affair  with kelly it would be equally  unsettling and they were never taking  care of her as a child because her  parents were off being astronauts i  don't want to go on about it but it  really took the air out of the episode  when he played the song because i'm  thinking this guy is set up to be the  potential future of the series and he  just feels like a reminder of one of the  worst turns the writers have made so  again i hope it does make sense that  they get into some of the nitty gritty  of what's behind it all rather than  treating it like it's just the thing  that happened that he can't let go of  and i do hope we get to see the new  character step up and fill the old one's  shoes because these really do have to be  their last missions on the other end ed  and danny's rivalry was a lot of fun and  some of the gravity stuff when what  feels like his soon-to-be second ex-wife  tries to throw her shoe at him and the  bride and groom on the wedding cake that  was all funny technically speaking the  space walk stuff looked great while  trying to make the cast look older  wasn't quite as effective i can mostly  overlook that though it was unconvincing  but not really distracting i like the  undercurrent of nasa changing and it  seems more autonomous than it was with  the dod in-house at the beginning of  season two we see that they're still  there but it didn't feel like they were  setting up the new general to be as  involved with margot's day-to-day like  bradford was  i hope this is an indication that we  won't have guns on the moon part 2 and  there's something new and exciting  happening on the red planet one detail  that i couldn't shake was that the  ending of season 2 shows someone  standing on mars in 1995  which indicates that the dates will get  pushed up that makes the nerva engine  stuff feel more critical and makes you  wonder how sergey and his team will get  ready if they're having trouble with  their engine and i didn't mention it  before but margot's situation seems  really concerning you can kind of see  how she's trying to do what's best for  humanity overall but it doesn't feel  like it's going to end well overall the  race does sound exciting and i'm anxious  to see what happens when they get to  mars i speculated on that some in my  trailer breakdown and there wasn't much  here to expand on that yet for all my  nitpicks i'm confident that this will be  an enjoyable season there's plenty to  like in this opening episode and i have  faith that things will fall into place i  think that's probably a good place to  leave things let me know in the comments  what you thought about the premiere what  you think about the trajectory of this  season so far and check back because i  will be doing a detailed breakdown of  all the alternate timeline changes  please like this video if you enjoyed it  please subscribe to my channel if you  haven't already and thanks for watching  i'll talk to you soon

